{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0a", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Dual Nature of Matter - Test 1", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d92346b3"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d92345c0", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234558", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "Two particles move at right angle to each other. Their de Broglie wavelengths are <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> respectively. The particles suffer perfectly inelastic collision. The de Broglie wavelength <math><mi>&#955;</mi></math> of the final particle, is given by:"}, "options": [{"id": "837d26", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234559"}, {"id": "5e24d6", "text": "<math><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923455a"}, {"id": "803195", "text": "<math><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923455b"}, {"id": "53db78", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923455c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923455d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c6843"], "question": {"text": "Which of the following statement is not true about stopping potential $\\left(\\mathrm{V}_0\\right)$ ?"}, "options": [{"id": "ab81ca", "text": "It is $1 /$ e times the maximum kinetic energy of electrons emitted.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923455e"}, {"id": "030df7", "text": "It increases with increase in intensity of the incident light.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923455f"}, {"id": "8393d9", "text": "It depends on the nature of emitter material.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234560"}, {"id": "26b0b3", "text": "It depends upon frequency of the incident light.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234561"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234562", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e3"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a6"], "question": {"text": "An oil drop of the radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> with a density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>cm</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;is held stationary under a constant electric field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>55</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> in the Millikan&#39;s oil drop experiment. What is the number of excess electrons that the oil drop will possess? <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mtext>consider</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>81</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math>."}, "options": [{"id": "5b4c01", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>48</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>11</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234563"}, {"id": "12cc49", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>73</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234564"}, {"id": "42c465", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>17</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234565"}, {"id": "741fd6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>73</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>12</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234566"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234567", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e4c"], "question": {"text": "The energy $E$ and momentum $p$ of a moving body of mass $m$ are related by some equation. Given that c represents the speed of light, identify the correct equation"}, "options": [{"id": "f52124", "text": "$\\mathrm{E}^2=\\mathrm{pc}^2+\\mathrm{m}^2 \\mathrm{c}^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234568"}, {"id": "7b3466", "text": "$\\mathrm{E}^2=\\mathrm{p}^2 \\mathrm{c}^2+\\mathrm{m}^2 \\mathrm{c}^2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234569"}, {"id": "671de0", "text": "$\\mathrm{E}^2=\\mathrm{pc}^2+\\mathrm{m}^2 \\mathrm{c}^4$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923456a"}, {"id": "ad09a4", "text": "$\\mathrm{E}^2=\\mathrm{p}^2 \\mathrm{c}^2+\\mathrm{m}^2 \\mathrm{c}^4$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923456b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923456c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60d2d1d0185d7a32fa320536"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.</p><p><strong>Assertion A :</strong> The photoelectric effect does not take&nbsp;place, if the energy of the incident radiation is less than the work function of a metal.</p><p><strong>Reason R :</strong> Kinetic energy of the photoelectrons is zero, if the energy of the incident radiation is equal to the work function of a metal.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "4521e3", "text": "Both A&nbsp;and R&nbsp;are correct and R&nbsp;is the correct explanation of A", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923456d"}, {"id": "351b14", "text": "Both A&nbsp;and R&nbsp;are correct but R&nbsp;is not the correct explanation of A", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923456e"}, {"id": "0694a9", "text": "A is correct but R&nbsp;is not correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923456f"}, {"id": "19db44", "text": "A is not correct but R is correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234570"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234571", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4706"], "question": {"text": "<p>The difference between threshold wavelengths for two metal surfaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> having work function&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#981;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>eV</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#981;</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>eV</mi></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>nm</mi></math>&nbsp;is:</p><p>{Given, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1242</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math>}</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "b57dbe", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>540</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234572"}, {"id": "764e31", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>276</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234573"}, {"id": "900a53", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>264</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234574"}, {"id": "0f6200", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>138</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234575"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234576", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["6176f3f46581241c7072ae65"], "question": {"text": "Consider two separate ideal gases of electrons and protons having same number of particles. The temperature of both the gases are same. The ratio of the uncertainty in determining the position of an electron to that of a proton is proportional to:"}, "options": [{"id": "563590", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234577"}, {"id": "0f6407", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234578"}, {"id": "67192c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234579"}, {"id": "8f58cc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923457a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923457b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60d2d1d1185d7a32fa320586"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52b7"], "question": {"text": "When the wavelength of radiation falling on a metal is changed from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>500</mn><mi>nm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>to</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>200</mn><mi>nm</mi></math>, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons becomes three times larger. The work function of the metal is close to:"}, "options": [{"id": "ffbcf6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>81</mn><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923457c"}, {"id": "928447", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923457d"}, {"id": "31b650", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>52</mn><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923457e"}, {"id": "dac1aa", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>61</mn><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d923457f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f132ee0e063d9234580", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205a2"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475f"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4715"], "question": {"text": "An electron of mass ' m ' with an initial velocity $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{v}}=\\mathrm{v}_0 \\hat{i}\\left(\\mathrm{v}_0\\gt0\\right)$ enters an electric field $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{E}}=-\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{o}} \\hat{\\mathrm{k}}$. If the initial de Broglie wavelength is $\\lambda_0$, the value after time t would be"}, "options": [{"id": "1d9ffc", "text": "$\\frac{\\lambda_0}{\\sqrt{1+\\frac{\\mathrm{e}^2 \\mathrm{E}_0{ }^2 \\mathrm{t}^2}{\\mathrm{~m}^2 \\mathrm{v}_0{ }^2}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f